WEBVTT

0
00:00.190 --> 00:01.023
<v Krista>In this video,</v>

1
00:01.023 --> 00:02.370
we're talking about absolute value,

2
00:02.370 --> 00:04.060
and the most important thing to remember

3
00:04.060 --> 00:06.370
about absolute value is that it just means

4
00:06.370 --> 00:07.940
distance from the origin.

5
00:07.940 --> 00:10.070
So I've got some examples here of the absolute value

6
00:10.070 --> 00:11.140
of different expressions.

7
00:11.140 --> 00:13.430
This first one is the absolute value of two.

8
00:13.430 --> 00:15.937
So these two lines are called absolute value brackets

9
00:15.937 --> 00:17.890
or absolute value bars.

10
00:17.890 --> 00:19.430
And what this tells us is that we're taking

11
00:19.430 --> 00:21.630
the absolute value of whatever's inside them,

12
00:21.630 --> 00:24.250
in this case the absolute value of two.

13
00:24.250 --> 00:26.720
So all we want to do is look at our number line.

14
00:26.720 --> 00:29.760
We always start here from the origin at zero,

15
00:29.760 --> 00:31.850
and we have positive two on the number line.

16
00:31.850 --> 00:34.050
So we're just going to go over to positive two,

17
00:34.050 --> 00:36.130
one, two, and we'll end up here.

18
00:36.130 --> 00:38.620
So how far is two from the origin?

19
00:38.620 --> 00:40.643
Well it's two units away from the origin,

20
00:40.643 --> 00:43.470
so the absolute value of two is just two

21
00:43.470 --> 00:45.460
because remember, absolute value of something

22
00:45.460 --> 00:48.110
is the distance of that thing from the origin.

23
00:48.110 --> 00:50.670
If we want to take the absolute value of negative two,

24
00:50.670 --> 00:52.810
we want to figure out how far negative two is

25
00:52.810 --> 00:54.300
away from the origin.

26
00:54.300 --> 00:57.420
So we start here at the origin, and we go to negative two.

27
00:57.420 --> 01:01.510
So one, two units to get to negative two.

28
01:01.510 --> 01:03.820
So this is going to be equal to two.

29
01:03.820 --> 01:05.520
In other words, whenever you have a value

30
01:05.520 --> 01:08.360
inside absolute value bars, the result is always

31
01:08.360 --> 01:10.690
going to be positive because the absolute value bars

32
01:10.690 --> 01:13.180
just tell you to take the distance from the origin,

33
01:13.180 --> 01:15.311
and whether you go to the right in the positive direction

34
01:15.311 --> 01:17.750
or to the left in the negative direction,

35
01:17.750 --> 01:20.720
distance from the origin is always still going to be positive.

36
01:20.720 --> 01:22.750
Now what happens when we have an expression like this?

37
01:22.750 --> 01:26.290
We have three minus four inside our absolute value bars.

38
01:26.290 --> 01:28.800
So this is the absolute value of three minus four.

39
01:28.800 --> 01:30.450
Well we have to simplify what's inside

40
01:30.450 --> 01:32.650
of our absolute value bars first.

41
01:32.650 --> 01:34.510
So three minus four is negative one,

42
01:34.510 --> 01:37.790
so this is going to be the absolute value of negative one.

43
01:37.790 --> 01:39.700
And just as we did here with the absolute value

44
01:39.700 --> 01:41.830
of negative two, we start at the origin,

45
01:41.830 --> 01:45.570
and we want to go over to negative one like this.

46
01:45.570 --> 01:47.850
We moved one unit away,

47
01:47.850 --> 01:51.240
so that means that the absolute value of negative one is one

48
01:51.240 --> 01:54.470
because negative one is just one unit away from the origin.

49
01:54.470 --> 01:56.940
If we have the opposite of the absolute value

50
01:56.940 --> 02:00.080
of positive four, well, we have to do what's inside

51
02:00.080 --> 02:01.600
the absolute value bars first.

52
02:01.600 --> 02:04.360
The absolute value of four or the distance of four

53
02:04.360 --> 02:06.310
from the origin is just going to be four

54
02:06.310 --> 02:08.809
because we would start here at the origin,

55
02:08.809 --> 02:13.809
and we would go over to four, one, two, three, four units

56
02:14.060 --> 02:16.030
to get to positive four.

57
02:16.030 --> 02:18.350
So the absolute value of four is just going to be four.

58
02:18.350 --> 02:21.960
So this part right here is going to simplify to four.

59
02:21.960 --> 02:23.850
So what we're going to get is we're going to leave

60
02:23.850 --> 02:26.900
the negative sign, and then we're going to get four here.

61
02:26.900 --> 02:29.034
So it's still negative four because this negative

62
02:29.034 --> 02:31.210
is outside of our absolute value bars,

63
02:31.210 --> 02:33.794
so we have to evaluate the absolute value of four first.

64
02:33.794 --> 02:35.900
We know that that's just four,

65
02:35.900 --> 02:38.300
and then we keep the negative sign out in front.